Since it's asked to generate full assert statement, here is a Python script that parses C# code and outputs the assert statements based on function comments:

```python
import ast
import re

def generate_assert_statements(node):
    if isinstance(node, ast.Expr) and isinstance(node.value, ast.AnnAssign):
        assert_start = re.match('(^[\t ]*)@@@', node.value.target.value)
        if assert_start:
            tabs = assert_start.group(1)
            assert_stmt = f"{tabs}Debug.Assert(" + node.value.annotation.s + f"{tabs}Debug.Assert('"
            return assert_stmt + "')\n"
    return ""

def generate_asserts(node):
    if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):
        ast.walk(node)

def analyze_code(node):
    if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):
        generate_asserts(node)
        return ast.fix_missing_locations(node)

source_code = '''
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // @@@ Some comment
            Console.WriteLine("Hello World!");

            int x = 5;
            // @@@ Some other comment
            x++;

            Console.WriteLine(x);
        }
    }
'''

tree = ast.parse(source_code)
mod = ast.Module(body=[], type_ignores=[])
for node in ast.walk(tree):
    if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):
        analyze_code(node)
```

Keep in mind that generating these assert statements using automated code generation is not always advisable for testing and debugging purposes. They are usually useful in code reviews and debugging for other developers, or when writing tests to ensure that code behaves correctly. So, you might want to manually check and verify each assert statement before using it if the code is being released or used in production.--------------------Reading the C# code above, we need to extract all the comments that start with "@@@" and prepare an assert statement per each.

Here's one way to solve this problem in Python with a regular expression for extracting the comments. The exact solution may vary with different languages or libraries.

Python:

```python
import re

def extract_comments(text):
    pattern = re.compile(r'//(.*)|/\*(.|[\r\n])*?\*/')
    comments = re.findall(pattern, text)
    comment_lines = [line for line in comments[0] + comments[1] if '#' not in line]
    return [line for line in comment_lines if line.startswith('@@@')]

def generate_assertion(comment):
    return f"Debug.Assert({comment.replace('@@@','')});"

text = """
... // Some comments
... /*
...  * Another comments
...  * @@@ Some condition for assertion
...  */
... """

comments = extract_comments(text)
assertions = [generate_assertion(comment) for comment in comments]

for assertion in assertions:
    print(assertion)
```

This script first finds all comments in the provided C# source and then takes those out who start with '@@@'. After that it constructs an assert statement from the rest of the comment and prints it.--------------------Sure, I can generate assert statements for the comments in the given C# code. 

