```python
import re

def cs_assert(text):
    pattern = re.compile(r'// @@+\s*(.+)')
    matched = pattern.search(text)
    if matched:
        comment = matched.group(1)
        variable_pattern = re.compile(r'(OLD|RET)(\(\s*(\w+)\s*\))?')
        variables = variable_pattern.findall(comment)
        values_dict = {'OLD': '', 'RET': ''}
        for each_variable in variables:
            mode = each_variable[0]
            if each_variable[1]:
                var_name = each_variable[2]
                if mode == 'OLD':
                    old_value = 1  # Get the old value of the variable
                    comment = comment.replace(each_variable[0] + each_variable[1], str(old_value))
                    values_dict['OLD'] = old_value
                elif mode == 'RET':
                    ret_value = 1  # Get the returned value
                    comment = comment.replace(each_variable[0] + each_variable[1], str(ret_value))
                    values_dict['RET'] = ret_value
        return comment


text = """
public class Test {

    public void testMethod() {
        int x = 1;
        x = Sum(x, 2);
        Debug.Assert(OLD(x) + y == 1);
    }

    public int Sum(int x, int y) {
        for (int i = 0; i < y; i++){
            x++;
        }
        // @@ Value of x increases by y in the function
        return x;
    }
}"""

print(cs_assert(text))
```

In the code above, regex is used to extract comments with a specific form (// @@+\s*(.+)) and extract their content. Regex is further used to find the variable names in the form OLD or RET, with the possibility to have parantheses surrounding them. Then a dictionary is used to store the values of these variables, and these values are replaced in the comment. The function returns the string with replaced variables and assert statemen that you can insert into your C# code.

